1
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:16,410
 I'd beat you.

2
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:18,980
 Ha-ha.

3
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:24,350
 Tell me, Grandpa Power.

4
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,830
 Why can't you play football with us?

5
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,900
 Would you like me to tell you a story?

6
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:32,210
 My story will answer your question, Peter.

7
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:39,200
 Once upon a time, long, long ago, in ancient Greece, there was a strange creature.

8
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:50,010
 Never seen a beast like you before.

9
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:52,070
 A mixture of woman, lion, and eagle.

10
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:53,030
 What's your name?

11
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:54,610
 Take care, Oedipus.

12
00:00:54,610 --> 00:00:56,570
 I am the one who asks the questions.

13
00:00:56,810 --> 00:00:59,070
 And if you don't know the right answers, I shall eat you.

14
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:00,880
 I am the Sphinx.

15
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:02,100
 So that's who you are.

16
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,500
 Well, then ask me a riddle. You'll never eat me.

17
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:15,800
 On four legs in the morning, two at noon, and three in set...

18
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:16,690
 A tortoise?

19
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,310
 A giraffe.

20
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:19,300
 No, no, no, that's not it.

21
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:27,380
 The stick. A stick. It's a man, that's the answer.

22
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:30,830
 In the morning of his life, a baby crawls on all fours.

23
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:32,890
 Then later, he walks on two legs.

24
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:35,900
 And in the evening of his life, he walks with the help of a stick.

25
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:38,060
 A third leg. That's right, isn't it?

26
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:50,250
 Silly old spit jumping over a cliff because someone guesses her riddle.

27
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:55,720
 And I walk on three legs, children, which is why I don't play football.

28
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,120
 Hello, Grandpa.

29
00:01:58,430 --> 00:01:59,820
 Why do people have to get old?

30
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,280
 Well, Peter, our bodies get tired.

31
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:05,470
 They slow down with age and they don't function so well.

32
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:07,400
 For instance, look at my cut finger.

33
00:02:07,810 --> 00:02:10,550
 It takes ten times longer than yours to heal.

34
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:15,700
 And every second of the day, there are thousands upon thousands of cells which die and have to be replaced.

35
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:17,110
 Grandpa, that's funny.

36
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:20,870
 Do you mean to say that cells get born and die just like people do?

37
00:02:21,870 --> 00:02:23,330
 Of course, my little one.

38
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:25,390
 Look at the skin on the back of your hand.

39
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:27,210
 It's made up of dead cells.

40
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:51,910
 You're being such a tearing hurry, young man.

41
00:02:52,410 --> 00:02:52,850
 Here.

42
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:56,310
 Something must come down, sir, they say.

43
00:02:56,390 --> 00:02:56,870
 Just watch.

44
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:08,830
 They are dead cells replaced from underneath.

45
00:03:09,270 --> 00:03:11,680
 And that's why skin never wears out.

46
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,140
 It's always being renewed.

47
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:15,200
 Cells make your nails grow.

48
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:18,420
 Other cells make your hair get longer.

49
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,860
 And cells heal all your cuts and scratches, too.

50
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:25,060
 Of course, I'm talking about cells that are doing their job properly.

51
00:03:25,550 --> 00:03:33,200
 And there's a great deal going on inside your body, repairs and renovations you can't see, to say nothing of the battles to defeat invading germs.

52
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:42,390
 There's no doubt about it, we owe our cells a great deal. Cells cause growth. It's like a miracle.

53
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:51,970
 If cells are always being replaced, then there's no reason for anyone to get old, is there?

54
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:59,040
 You've got a short memory, haven't you? Remember I once told you that cells work more slowly as time goes by?

55
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:05,960
 They take longer to divide, and nerve cells die in their thousands each day without ever being replaced.

56
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:14,790
 What do you think, Grandpa?

57
00:04:15,170 --> 00:04:17,960
 Only a couple of years to go and you'll be 90.

58
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,700
 I can't imagine what it must be like to be as old as you.

59
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:23,660
 There's so much more to look back on than there is to look forward to.

60
00:04:24,010 --> 00:04:26,270
 Oh, I have a great deal to look forward.

61
00:04:27,150 --> 00:04:29,230
 My future is in you, children.

62
00:04:29,230 --> 00:04:37,310
 One day, when my chromosomes were young, they combined with my wife's chromosomes to make a new life.

63
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:38,820
 A child.

64
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:45,950
 That's how I handed over my baton in the relay race to eternity.

65
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:52,840
 Your parents did the same, with the result that there's a bit of me in you, my grandchildren.

66
00:04:55,550 --> 00:05:02,960
 One day, when you have children of your own, they will go on with a little bit of you and of me in them, carrying on my line.

67
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:08,940
 The instructions of our genetic code really are immortal, because they can be handed down forever.

68
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:12,440
 That's why I call it a relay race to eternity.

69
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,610
 I'd like to go in now, I'm feeling a little tired.

70
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:37,250
 We can't go on patching it up any longer.

71
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:40,030
 You may as well put out an order for materials to build a new one.

72
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:44,370
 Doing it?

73
00:05:44,710 --> 00:05:46,610
 Why ever not, for heaven's sake?

74
00:05:46,790 --> 00:05:50,950
 Now look, he's a U and you're an A, so you're supposed to match up, aren't you?

75
00:05:51,010 --> 00:05:53,710
 I refuse to join up with him. How do I know where he's been, hmm?

76
00:05:53,950 --> 00:05:55,450
 Why can't you be reasonable?

77
00:05:59,710 --> 00:06:07,790
 This ribosome decided to go in the blink, just when they're screaming for protein.

78
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:11,650
 Well, tough luck. I'm surprised. The whole thing hasn't blown up long before now anyway.

79
00:06:11,970 --> 00:06:14,150
 The cell's going from bad to worse.

80
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:22,690
 Look what's happening, Chief. Things keep breaking down. Shouldn't we try to mend them or something? It's serious.

81
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:31,860
 What do you expect? We're getting old. We all succumb to old age in the end, you know.

82
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:37,280
 Even cells wear out eventually. The defense system, too. No one's immune to old age.

83
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:52,030
 Oh, can I go on now?

84
00:06:52,030 --> 00:06:57,090
 Old age affects the circulation too, so let's take a look at the corpuscles.

85
00:06:57,090 --> 00:07:12,900
 Old Professor Globus has called it a day.

86
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:19,350
 This decay is enough to make a fellow feel quite tired out.

87
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:27,310
 Daddy? Daddy, wake up.

88
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:30,120
 The children have come to pay you a little visit.

89
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:38,050
 Who are you, grandpapa?

90
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:40,090
 I could be better.

91
00:07:40,090 --> 00:07:42,790
 Can't expect much at my age.

92
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:48,080
 I'm asleep most of the time, but I don't feel sad in the least, you know.

93
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:54,540
 When my time comes, I'll go in peace, because I know that I'll go on living in you, my descendants.

94
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:59,000
 Do you know that you get the shape of your nose or the color of your eyes from me?

95
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:02,590
 That's my legacy to you, in the shape of your chromosomes.

96
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:03,460
 Yes.

97
00:08:04,420 --> 00:08:06,220
 Why should I be sad?

98
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:09,060
 I shall live on in all of your personalities.

99
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:11,100
 There's a bit of me in each of you.

100
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:44,520
 There's a leg up!

101
00:08:45,210 --> 00:08:47,000
 I'm supposed to get out of here!

102
00:08:47,570 --> 00:08:49,980
 Get off my head for a start!

103
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:56,170
 Carbon Dioxide Bubbles are about to be buried under a p-xygen!

104
00:08:56,170 --> 00:09:02,080
 Fancy meeting all of you here!

105
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:05,890
 Hey, when's you're not supposed to get blown out on that last breath?

106
00:09:05,890 --> 00:09:09,690
 That's right! But the lungs simply aren't strong enough to blow us out!

107
00:09:09,690 --> 00:09:13,250
 We won't get out now!

108
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:18,150
 That child of mine got to! Lightyear, where are you?

109
00:09:18,150 --> 00:09:33,390
 Test Carbon Dioxide, don't you?

110
00:09:33,390 --> 00:09:35,380
 And yet you just can't get rid of it!

111
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:37,380
 It seems such a shame!

112
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,330
 Even the heart is slowing down!

113
00:09:39,330 --> 00:09:55,590
 Purity forces are sleeping on the job! Let's get 'em!

114
00:09:55,590 --> 00:09:56,650
 Charge!

115
00:09:56,650 --> 00:10:00,690
 The old man's heart no longer pumps blood as strongly as it did.

116
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:02,450
 The heart valves are getting leaky.

117
00:10:06,690 --> 00:10:10,710
 Our station of the heart can no longer keep up a healthy pulse rate.

118
00:10:10,930 --> 00:10:13,390
 Oh dear, 50 beats per minute.

119
00:10:13,710 --> 00:10:15,850
 That's far too slow, oh dear.

120
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:38,230
 Urgent supply to the brain is simply not enough.

121
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:40,890
 We'll have to speed up the rate of breathing and the heartbeat.

122
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:44,980
 Hurry, this is urgent.

123
00:10:46,630 --> 00:10:50,150
 Hurry up.

124
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:56,000
 Oh, I've got an urgent message for the heart.

125
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:58,120
 It's from the brain.

126
00:10:58,440 --> 00:10:58,680
 Here.

127
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:07,720
 It's the heartbeat, from 50 to 65 beats per minute, to a down to 40 already.

128
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:10,560
 It just won't work.

129
00:11:10,890 --> 00:11:13,670
 The heart isn't getting enough oxygen to make it beat any faster.

130
00:11:14,780 --> 00:11:17,390
 We've done for it, the heartbeat won't accelerate.

131
00:11:21,190 --> 00:11:24,350
 Up, speedy.

132
00:11:25,710 --> 00:11:27,840
 Take this to the heart immediately.

133
00:11:28,730 --> 00:11:30,580
 Okay, I'm on my way.

134
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:32,290
 But I might not arrive.

135
00:11:32,550 --> 00:11:34,230
 I'm utterly worn out.

136
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:40,950
 The nerve signals are not getting through.

137
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:48,210
 Oh, this is the end.

138
00:11:48,450 --> 00:11:50,630
 I'm long overdue for retirement.

139
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,400
 Something yellow showing on the left.

140
00:12:57,470 --> 00:13:02,380
 Can't use information like that.

141
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:03,520
 It's far too vague.

142
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,940
 I require much, much more precision in sensory data.

143
00:13:07,570 --> 00:13:09,010
 Well, pass on the signals anyway.

144
00:13:09,330 --> 00:13:10,370
 They'll learn in time.

145
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:32,780
 There's a funny sounding order from the brain.

146
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:36,980
 Move the hand sort of to the left to grab a sort of object.

147
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:37,840
 What's this?

148
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:40,580
 I know it's all very confusing to begin with.

149
00:13:40,870 --> 00:13:42,190
 But he'll learn quickly enough.

150
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:44,330
 You see, a baby is born with all the equipment.

151
00:13:44,770 --> 00:13:48,230
 But it takes him some time to learn how to use it to recognise colours and shapes.

152
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:50,580
 and to coordinate his movements.

153
00:13:50,580 --> 00:13:52,890
 This is what we call perceptual development.

154
00:13:52,890 --> 00:13:54,670
 So send the message.

155
00:13:54,670 --> 00:13:59,650
 How's the bloodstream?

156
00:13:59,650 --> 00:14:02,550
 Water, who'll buy my fresh water?

157
00:14:02,550 --> 00:14:04,270
 Very best quality calcium.

158
00:14:04,270 --> 00:14:06,220
 Going cheap, best quality calcium,

159
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:09,010
 just what you need for healthy bones and a strong skeleton.

160
00:14:09,010 --> 00:14:12,270
 Water, who'll buy my fresh water?

161
00:14:12,270 --> 00:14:14,650
 Water, my fresh...

162
00:14:14,650 --> 00:14:20,590
 Every single child needs calcium.

163
00:14:20,590 --> 00:14:22,490
 You can't have healthy bones without it.

164
00:14:22,490 --> 00:14:24,560
 And water, what about water?

165
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:27,830
 You've got to keep a baby well hydrated, haven't you, huh?

166
00:14:27,830 --> 00:14:29,120
 Ha!

167
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:32,400
 Obstructing the way, you two.

168
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:36,130
 These proteins are urgently needed for big muscle building projects.

169
00:14:36,130 --> 00:14:37,630
 Out of the way, out of the way!

170
00:14:37,630 --> 00:14:38,790
 Aha!

171
00:14:38,790 --> 00:14:41,820
 You're so busy.

172
00:14:41,820 --> 00:14:46,940
 So do I, and there's always such a lot to do, with a new life starting out.

173
00:14:46,940 --> 00:14:49,070
 The lymphocyte patrol.

174
00:14:49,070 --> 00:14:51,510
 Hello!

175
00:14:51,510 --> 00:14:58,270
 There are so many things to do, you know, like teaching the Defence Force smart germs, for example.

176
00:14:58,270 --> 00:15:00,640
 The Sight Patrol know that.

177
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:05,440
 Oh yes, they do, because they're here on transfer from the bloodstream of the mother.

178
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:06,940
 It's a stopgap arrangement.

179
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:13,470
 They are here to keep the bodies safe from germs and viruses, while the baby is busy training his own police force.

180
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:15,690
 They train the policemen?

181
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,600
 In the thymus gland. That's the body's police college.

182
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:24,360
 I'd like to introduce Captain Peter and Lieutenant Claire of the Lymphocyte Patrol.

183
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:30,060
 They have been seconded to us from the maternal organism to assist us in setting up our very own security force.

184
00:15:30,890 --> 00:15:34,950
 These experienced officers will instruct us in counter-infiltration techniques.

185
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:38,700
 Daphilococcus.

186
00:15:39,150 --> 00:15:40,510
 And the streptococcus.

187
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:41,820
 Shoot them on sight.

188
00:15:42,260 --> 00:15:44,220
 The flu virus is extremely dangerous.

189
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:46,520
 Now, who can identify this friendly germ?

190
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:47,940
 That's E. coli!

191
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:50,240
 And death to all the others!

192
00:15:51,190 --> 00:15:52,600
 Daddy!

193
00:15:53,730 --> 00:15:55,920
 It's so good to be home.

194
00:15:56,530 --> 00:15:58,430
 And how's my big son, huh?

195
00:15:59,230 --> 00:16:12,910
 You have to be an astronaut when I grow older.

196
00:16:17,870 --> 00:16:24,270
 And here is the apogee of the orbit.

197
00:16:24,470 --> 00:16:27,430
 I would now like you to calculate the following using the data provided.

198
00:16:27,790 --> 00:16:29,510
 Duration of orbital insertion burn,

199
00:16:29,790 --> 00:16:30,590
 mass of payload,

200
00:16:30,890 --> 00:16:32,010
 and altitude of perigee.

201
00:16:32,550 --> 00:16:34,170
 On the surface of the Earth,

202
00:16:34,270 --> 00:16:36,990
 The heart pumps blood downwards with the assistance of gravity,

203
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:39,290
 towards against the gravitational pull.

204
00:16:39,850 --> 00:16:42,650
 Now, what happens in free fall when the body is weightless?

205
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:45,380
 Does the heart beat faster or slower?

206
00:16:48,470 --> 00:16:50,890
 News, this is your last day of theory.

207
00:16:51,030 --> 00:16:53,230
 You'll be happy to know, practical begins tomorrow.

208
00:16:53,230 --> 00:16:57,710
 At 0600 hours, we blast off for the space relay station, Haythor.

209
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:00,440
 Then, transfer to the starship Horus.

210
00:17:00,760 --> 00:17:05,640
 The Horus with iron jets, 100 times more powerful than the biggest atomic motor in space.

211
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:07,800
 Any volunteers for the great adventure?

212
00:17:08,990 --> 00:17:20,230
 Seven, four, two, one, but it was only a muscle contract.

213
00:17:20,250 --> 00:17:20,530
 Zero.

214
00:17:21,990 --> 00:17:26,260
 Nothing's wrong.

215
00:17:26,770 --> 00:17:28,770
 Why should I feel so heavy?

216
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:39,880
 Why do we all suddenly weigh a ton?

217
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:42,140
 I was planning a wonderful virus infection.

218
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:44,880
 That gets you down, doesn't it?

219
00:17:44,880 --> 00:18:00,380
 Spheric pressure is now restored in the cabin.

220
00:18:01,370 --> 00:18:05,710
 Exercise caution in releasing straps, as we shall shortly be in zero gravity.

221
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:09,620
 When the motor stops burning, we shall be in orbit.

222
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:13,730
 Thrust to zero.

223
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:14,580
 Now!

224
00:18:17,810 --> 00:18:22,600
 ...like a feather!

225
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:41,920
 ...is it a guess.

226
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:44,940
 ...the law of gravity is being repealed.

227
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:47,860
 Enjoy life without the law of gravity?

228
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:49,930
 It's really fun!

229
00:18:49,930 --> 00:19:03,510
 ...sure is now on atmosphere.

230
00:19:03,510 --> 00:19:11,940
 Beginning, chaps.

231
00:19:11,940 --> 00:19:15,330
 We're gonna take over the space shuttle, and no mistake.

232
00:19:18,470 --> 00:19:21,470
 Goodness me, you've got a cold!

233
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:23,350
 And that could be very serious.

234
00:19:23,350 --> 00:19:26,780
 We have a virus on board, even though the cabin was disinfected before blast-off.

235
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:29,030
 We must act without delay.

236
00:19:29,030 --> 00:19:31,500
 Firstly, to tackle the virus inside.

237
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:34,820
 Dose of Alpha Interferon to activate your bodily defenses.

238
00:19:34,820 --> 00:19:37,540
 Secondly, get into your spacesuits.

239
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:47,530
 ...airlock now.

240
00:19:47,530 --> 00:19:53,590
 Infection completed.

241
00:19:53,590 --> 00:20:00,070
 Air pressure is restored to one atmosphere.

242
00:20:00,070 --> 00:20:05,930
 You can check all the viewing ports.

243
00:20:05,930 --> 00:20:07,730
 The station should be clearly visible now.

244
00:20:07,730 --> 00:20:09,720
 Right, Colonel.

245
00:20:09,720 --> 00:20:12,750
 There she is.

246
00:20:12,750 --> 00:20:13,820
 Isn't that fantastic?

247
00:20:13,820 --> 00:20:35,120
 ...introduction.

248
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:37,340
 He's the leading scientist of our century

249
00:20:37,340 --> 00:20:41,050
 and the leader of our latest expedition.

250
00:20:41,050 --> 00:20:48,770
 You are at last the members of 200 carefully selected families

251
00:20:48,770 --> 00:20:51,470
 who will take part in man's greatest adventure,

252
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:53,610
 The exploration of interstellar space.

253
00:20:54,130 --> 00:20:58,900
 And I'm proud of the fact that men are going to the stars, not robots or computers.

254
00:20:59,820 --> 00:21:05,770
 In the early years of the 21st century, there were great strides made in the field of genetic engineering.

255
00:21:13,490 --> 00:21:15,790
 Standing physical specimens were produced.

256
00:21:15,790 --> 00:21:18,780
 The finest athletes were created.

257
00:21:20,350 --> 00:21:21,920
 The biggest brains.

258
00:21:23,010 --> 00:21:26,720
 As well as subhuman creatures destined to perform menial tasks.

259
00:21:27,460 --> 00:21:32,750
 There were even birdmen and fishmen in those benighted days.

260
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:36,480
 Power mad men ruled the world at that time.

261
00:21:37,230 --> 00:21:40,030
 Eventually, their excesses drew people to revolt.

262
00:21:41,810 --> 00:21:47,470
 A few ambitious scientists tried to achieve immortality and a blueprint for just government

263
00:21:47,470 --> 00:21:50,710
 by having their brains housed inside an enormous computer.

264
00:21:50,710 --> 00:21:55,240
 The machine was also supposed to provide an eternal storehouse for their psyches.

265
00:21:55,700 --> 00:22:01,140
 I'm sure you've all heard mention of the grand computer, but the grand computer was a failure.

266
00:22:01,600 --> 00:22:06,980
 It became a megalomaniac machine which believed itself to be all-powerful and superior to its creators.

267
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:10,460
 Bow down and worship me. I am the grand computer.

268
00:22:10,860 --> 00:22:14,540
 Men of flesh and blood defeated this machine and switched it off.

269
00:22:14,540 --> 00:22:21,630
 And now ordinary men and women made of flesh and blood are about to undertake the greatest adventure of all time.

270
00:22:21,960 --> 00:22:25,820
 At our beck and call, we have the most powerful computers ever created.

271
00:22:26,360 --> 00:22:30,120
 Should anyone need to draw information from the total sum of human knowledge,

272
00:22:30,380 --> 00:22:34,220
 then a simple implanted chip is all he needs to access this database.

273
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:38,560
 Yes, and yet the human brain out-thinks the computer.

274
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:43,800
 And we ordinary mortals with our puny lifespan of a hundred years at most,

275
00:22:44,290 --> 00:22:47,810
 we shall explore the stars, explore eternity.

276
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:53,000
 Our starship with its ion jet propulsion is incredibly fast.

277
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:58,530
 However, even if we could touch the speed of light, which is 300,000 kilometers per second,

278
00:22:58,950 --> 00:23:02,490
 It would take us a quarter of a million years to cross our own galaxy.

279
00:23:02,990 --> 00:23:07,020
 And ten times longer to voyage to Andromeda, our nearest galactic neighbor.

280
00:23:07,410 --> 00:23:11,040
 This means that it will take maybe a hundred, maybe a thousand years

281
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:15,740
 to explore only a few of the solar systems in the Bilky Way looking for a habitable planet.

282
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:17,840
 Of course, none of us will live to see that day.

283
00:23:17,840 --> 00:23:22,470
 No, the discovery will be made by the remote descendants of the people in this hall.

284
00:23:22,970 --> 00:23:27,150
 And on that day, humanity will have taken a gigantic leap forward.

285
00:23:27,790 --> 00:23:32,350
 Eventually, stately spaceliners will link Earth to her new colony.

286
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:38,580
 On that day, our species shall become the leading light of the space lanes.

287
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:44,060
 And on that distant day, lost to us in the furthest reaches of the future,

288
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:49,300
 the day when our children set foot on a new planet, we shall be with them.

289
00:23:49,300 --> 00:24:04,490
 We shall be there in the bodies of our descendants in their chromosomes,

290
00:24:04,710 --> 00:24:07,490
 which will have been transmitted from generation to generation.

291
00:24:08,450 --> 00:24:11,520
 Yes, our chromosomes will move us into the future.

